  "verdicts": {
    "priority1": "PARTIALLY WEAKENS / QUALIFIES the hypothesis: the layer-20 semantic probe clearly beats the top-k logit-lens controls (which only see the immediate next-token distribution) by a large margin, but is matched or beaten by the short-rollout controls with m>=3 tokens (short_rollout_m3 / short_rollout_m5). Honest reading: these rollout controls are literally the model's own first m tokens of the SAME deterministic greedy continuation, so for a dataset whose continuations are short on average (~16 tokens after sentence-boundary truncation), 3-5 tokens already cover a large fraction of the eventual sentence, and MiniLM sentence embeddings of a short prefix are already highly similar to the embedding of the full sentence it is a prefix of. This does not show the probe recovers nothing beyond immediate-token predictability in an absolute sense, but it does show that a strong, cheap, non-learned token-identity baseline (the literal next few greedily-decoded tokens) is very hard to beat with these short continuations, which is exactly the kind of control the protocol asked for to stress-test the original hypothesis. The probe DOES still convincingly beat controls that only see a single next-token distribution (top-k logit lens), which is the closer analogue to Future Lens-style token-identity probing.",
    "priority2": "Probe recoverability (cosine gain over the mean-embedding baseline) is clearly present at every scale tested (pythia-410m, pythia-1.0b, pythia-1.4b, pythia-2.8b) and is non-decreasing overall with size. Steering margin (semantic direction vs. matched-norm random direction) is positive at 4/4 scales tested. This is consistent with hypothesis (b): the effect is present across multiple Pythia scales and is not a fragile artifact of one specific model size. One caveat: Recall@5 dips at pythia-1.0b (0.687) relative to its neighbors in the scaling curve -- still far above chance (0.060) but a reminder that the trend is not perfectly monotonic at n=83 test examples per model; pythia-1.0b also has an unusual layer count (16 blocks) among the models tested, which affects which depth-fraction layer is compared.",
    "priority3": "Sampled decoding STRENGTHENS the hypothesis that the effect is not a pure greedy-decoding artifact: probe test cosine under sampling is 0.3508 vs 0.4283 under greedy (i.e. weaker, as expected, since sampled continuations are noisier targets), but remains clearly above the sampled mean-embedding baseline (0.2042). Margin over the strongest rollout control (m=5) is -0.2089 under greedy vs -0.2486 under sampled -- the probe-vs-strong-control picture is qualitatively similar.",
    "priority1_probe_beats_all_controls": false,
    "priority1_strong_outcome_achieved": false,
